  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The percentage of impurity in a batch of chemicals\n- The area of an equilateral triangle with a side of $2$\n- The amount of snowfall, in centimeters, in a randomly selected city in January 2022",
  "worked_solution": "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I  describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,100]$ (since the percentage can be anywhere between $0%$ and $100%$).\n\n- Statement II does *not* describe a continuous random variable. The area of an equilateral triangle with a given side length is not the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
